G color in a natural 4ct is not a flaw — it's the lowest grade that still reads as near-colorless in yellow gold. In white gold or platinum, G shows faint warmth. Most buyers set a G natural in yellow or rose gold to neutralize the warmth and get near-D color performance at the G price.

VS1 at 4ct is safely eye-clean when the inclusions sit away from the center table. The GIA report will identify inclusion type and position — always request the HD video before buying to confirm the inclusions aren't visible through the 11mm step-cut table.

The [D-VVS1 natural at $260,848](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29395591?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is a collector stone — absolute top grades at 4ct. The lab D-VVS1 at [$7,737](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29587315?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) delivers the same grade certification for 33.7x less.

