The [$7,737 D-VVS1](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29587315?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is the strongest value argument in the entire emerald cut market at any carat size. D color and VVS1 clarity at 4ct — for less than the cost of the cheapest natural 1.5ct ring. The setting choice at this price should reflect the stone's size: for daily wear the [$990 bezel](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/comfort-fit-bezel-set-solitaire-engagement-ring-in-14k-white-gold-by-james-allen-item-315709?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) protects the corners; for a showcase piece the [$4,625 platinum side-stone](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/bezel-set-emerald-cut-side-stone-diamond-engagement-ring-in-platinum-by-james-allen-item-304637?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) frames an 11mm stone correctly.

On VVS1 vs FL at this price: VVS1 is invisible to the naked eye and invisible under standard loupe inspection. FL means no inclusions even under 10x magnification by a trained grader. At 4ct in an everyday ring, VVS1 is more than enough.

FL at [$16,428](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29327214?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) carries the grade certification — worth the extra cost if the FL on the certificate matters to you, not because you'll see the difference wearing it.

> "The 4ct lab is the stone I show first now when a client comes in with any interest in this size. Not because it's cheaper — that's obvious — but because D-VVS1 quality simply doesn't exist in natural at anything near a reasonable price. The cheapest natural 4ct is $97K for G-VS1. The lab is $7,737 for D-VVS1. I let the client decide, but the data does all the work." — Farzana, diamond advisor

---

## The 12x Problem

The price ratio between natural and lab at 4ct reaches 12.6 times at the entry level. The [$97,688 natural G-VS1](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/20429165?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) and the [$7,737 lab D-VVS1](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29587315?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) are both 4ct emerald cuts.

The lab has better color (D vs G) and better clarity (VVS1 vs VS1). It costs $89,951 less.

This ratio is specific to 4ct. At 1ct, natural and lab are within 1.4x of each other in price. At 2ct, the gap widens.

At 4ct, it reaches 12.6x — the point at which natural rarity premiums, grade premiums, and market scarcity compound together.

[![Budget comparison at the 4ct natural vs lab crossover](/images/505082_M1_EMR_DIM_wht_0100CT_Y_Box3_003_1600X1600.avif)](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings?MetalType=all&Diamonds=Dia&ShapeID=Emerald&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)

